<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Barghawata</h1><p class="st-meta">744 CE – 1058 CE (approx.) · capital Tamesna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Berber confederation on Morocco's Atlantic coast that followed its own prophet and scripture for over three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Barghawata emerged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamesna">Tamesna</a> plain between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safi-morocco">Safi</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> revolt of 740 against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>. Their first leader, Tarif, took part in the revolt, and around 744 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salih-ibn-tarif">Salih ibn Tarif</a> proclaimed himself a prophet, founding a new religion for his people.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/salihids">Salih</a> gave the Barghawata a Berber-language scripture of eighty suras and distinct laws, including fasting in the month of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajab">Rajab</a>, prayers at different times and food taboos, according to the 11th-century geographer al-Bakri. His successors, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunus-ibn-ilyas">Yunus ibn Ilyas</a>, spread the faith by force. The confederation of farming and herding tribes fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banu-ifran-emirate">Banu Ifran emirate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> attacked them from 1058; their spiritual leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-ibn-yasin">Abdallah ibn Yasin</a> was killed fighting them in 1059, but the Barghawata were broken. Remnants were finally wiped out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in the mid-12th century.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/barghawata.jpg" alt="Barghawata" width="320" height="186"><figcaption>Image: Omar-toons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morocco_and_the_Maghreb_after_the_Berber_Revolt.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>744 CE – 1058 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tamesna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tarif, Salih ibn Tarif, Yunus ibn Ilyas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Barghawata religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Berghouata, Barghawata confederation</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=901&amp;focus=barghawata" aria-label="Barghawata on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4484 960 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Barghawata lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4804 1216L4799 1289"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4799 1289h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4804 1216h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4804 1216h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=901&amp;focus=barghawata">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

